as out  there  but foundation is the one that everybody  knows about what makes the series  relatable  is also what makes it somewhat dated now  i said it was a future history  and essentially what he did was he took  the roman empire  and applied the same sort of thing on a  bigger scale across the whole galaxy  he got the idea after reading the  history of the decline and fall of the  roman empire  which was a late 18th century historical  series by edward gibbons  it's relatable in that they're doing  things that humans do  things that they've done historically  but it feels dated because  you would expect that over 20 000 years  humanity  society ideas of government you would  expect that they would evolve in some  way  that's something i'll come back to at  the end but now let's look at the things  we know about asimov's galactic empire  it's in our galaxy the milky way it's  made up of about 25 million planets  and it's mentioned that the population  was 500 quadrillion residents  early on in the empire for reference a  quadrillion  is one thousand trillions the empire was  founded at around the year 10  000 ce on our calendar when the main  story picks up  the empire has been around for nearly 12  000 years  so the year would be something like 21  000 and change on our calendar  the colonization was made possible by  hyperspace travel  and this is the thing that kind of makes  this whole idea  and every other galactic empire story  that came after it  somewhat inevitable once you start to  imagine a time when technology  that can create things that make faster  than light travel  possible then it's only a matter of time  until people start to colonize all the  different planets  at least the ones that have breathable  atmosphere  in foundations empire there's only  humans there's no intelligent  alien species all these humans came from  earth but by this point in their history  they don't remember that anymore  we hear characters talk about the origin  question  and it's generally accepted that all  humans must have  evolved on one planet but there's not a  general consensus of what that planet is  or what happened to it  the empire is ruled by one person the  emperor  and he runs things from the capital  planet of trantor  which i'll talk about in a minute  there's another group of people called  the spacers who left earth and aren't  involved with the empire  but they're really not important at this  point in the story  so basically humans from earth spread  out to everywhere that they could  populations continue to multiply because  everyone else is a member of the empire  there are galactic standards so  everybody speaks the same language  their standard galactic time and the  standard galactic  day obviously all these planets would  have different orbits  different night and day cycles but they  all have a 24-hour day  and an agreed-upon calendar since we're  already 12  000 years on there's really no mention  of how they made these planets  livable in the main foundation series  at this point they're all basically like  different versions of earth  i mentioned that this story was written  in the 1940s that was a time where there  was a lot of technological advances  because of the war there was a lot of  new things that were developed but not  really on the consumer end  so when asimov was writing these stories  the idea of computers  especially things like home computers  portable computers  they were still decades away there's  really no mention of computers in the  original trilogy  although decades later when he came back  to write the sequels  he did update the technology to include  computers and other things like that  the other technological thing that might  seem weird especially considering  asimov's other big series is about them  is that there aren't any robots in the  main trilogy towards the end of his  career he did introduce  ties between the foundation and robot  series  they do exist in the same 